---
title: Neural ODE Parameter Estimation with GPU-PSO
author: Aditya Pandey
---

A 12-parameter SimpleChains network is fit to spiral ODE data (30 points on (0, 1.5)) so
that the neural ODE `du/dt = NN(u, θ)` reproduces the data. The problem is the neural ODE
experiment (Section 3.2) of the ParallelParticleSwarms.jl workshop paper
([ParallelParticleSwarms.jl#76](https://github.com/SciML/ParallelParticleSwarms.jl/pull/76)),
extended with the library's other PSO variants and compared at equal wall-clock time.

Twelve optimizers:

- **Gradient-based**: `Adam` and `LBFGS`, trained through the ODE solve with
  `QuadratureAdjoint`.
- **CPU global**: BlackBoxOptim's adaptive DE and NLopt's `GN_CRS2_LM`, on the same loss and
  box, stopped by their own `maxtime`.
- **PSO**: `SerialPSO` on the CPU; GPU-PSO through `parameter_estim_ode!` (all particles'
  ODEs solved as one GPU ensemble); `SyncPSOKernel` and `HybridPSO-LBFGS` on a fixed-step
  RK4 loss that runs inside the GPU kernels. The last two share loss and swarm, so their
  difference isolates the L-BFGS polish. `HybridPSO (restarts)` uses the same swarm and loss
  as short, repeated runs (100 swarm iterations, then 100 L-BFGS iterations) until the budget
  runs out, keeping the best.
- **Unreleased ParallelParticleSwarms features**: `SyncPSO local-best` is `SyncPSOKernel`
  with `local_best = true`, so each block of particles follows its own best
  ([ParallelParticleSwarms.jl#149](https://github.com/SciML/ParallelParticleSwarms.jl/pull/149)).
  `HybridPSO local-best` is `HybridPSO-LBFGS` on that swarm. `HybridPSO-LBFGS+NM` is
  `HybridPSO-LBFGS` with a Nelder-Mead polish of the best point after L-BFGS, capped at
  1,000 iterations per run so the polish fits in the budget
  ([ParallelParticleSwarms.jl#150](https://github.com/SciML/ParallelParticleSwarms.jl/pull/150)).
  These rows run on a fork branch that combines both pull requests.

## Protocol

Every optimizer gets the same wall-clock budget, `BUDGET` seconds, enforced against the
clock: Adam and L-BFGS stop through a callback, the CPU global methods through their own
`maxtime`, the GPU swarms are advanced in chunks with the best cost read back between
chunks, `HybridPSO` reserves its measured polish time out of the budget, and
`HybridPSO (restarts)` starts a restart only if its measured duration still fits.
`SerialPSO` has no resumable cache, so its iteration count is calibrated from a timed run
instead. An optimizer that converges early keeps its early result.

Each optimizer runs from `length(SEEDS)` fresh starts. The table reports, per optimizer:

- **Common loss** (median, best, worst over seeds): the returned parameters re-evaluated
  under one reference loss, `Tsit5` in `Float64` at `abstol = reltol = 1e-10`. This is the
  only column that ranks optimizers.
- **Own objective** of the median run: the optimizer's own solver at the parameters it
  returned. Every optimizer scores candidates with some ODE solver, and a swarm will
  exploit that solver's error if it can. The gap to the common loss is checked after the
  runs.
- **Iterations** completed and **evaluations** (ODE solves) performed, and the **median
  time**.

All GPU swarms use 10,000 particles; `SerialPSO` uses 512. `HybridPSO` is built on the same
`SyncPSOKernel` swarm, so its row is that swarm plus an L-BFGS polish of every particle, and
the difference between the two rows is what the polish adds.

## Setup

```julia
using Random; Random.seed!(0)
using SimpleChains, StaticArrays, OrdinaryDiffEq, SciMLSensitivity
using Optimization, OptimizationOptimisers, OptimizationOptimJL, Optimisers
using OptimizationBBO, OptimizationNLopt
using ParallelParticleSwarms, DiffEqGPU, CUDA, KernelAbstractions, Adapt
using Statistics, Printf, CairoMakie
CairoMakie.activate!()

const SciMLBase = Optimization.SciMLBase
const BACKEND = CUDABackend()
CUDA.allowscalar(false)
println("GPU: ", CUDA.name(CUDA.device()))

const NPARAMS = 12
const DATASIZE = 30
const BOX = 10.0f0
const BUDGET = 60.0           # seconds per optimizer per run
const SEEDS = 1:10

const NPART_GPU = 10_000     # every GPU swarm
const NPART_CPU = 512        # SerialPSO
const CHUNK_KERNEL = 50         # kernel-PSO iterations between clock checks
const CHUNK_GPUPSO = 1          # GPU-PSO iterations between clock checks
const CAL_ITERS = 10         # SerialPSO calibration run
const LOCAL_MAXITERS = 10       # Hybrid L-BFGS iterations per particle
```

## Problem

```julia
const U0 = @SVector Float32[2.0, 0.0]
const TSPAN = (0.0f0, 1.5f0)
tsteps = range(TSPAN[1], TSPAN[2], length = DATASIZE)

trueODE(u, p, t) = (((u .^ 3)' * @SMatrix Float32[-0.1 2.0; -2.0 -0.1])')
data = Array(solve(ODEProblem(trueODE, U0, TSPAN), Tsit5(), saveat = tsteps))

sc = SimpleChain(
    static(2), Activation(x -> x .^ 3),
    TurboDense{true}(tanh, static(2)), TurboDense{true}(identity, static(2))
)
p_nn = Vector{Float32}(SimpleChains.init_params(sc; rng = Random.default_rng()))
p_static = SVector{NPARAMS, Float32}(p_nn...)

# Hand-written forward for the GPU paths and the reference: SimpleChains emits CPU
# intrinsics that do not lower to PTX.
function mlp(u, θ)
    v = u .^ 3
    h1 = tanh(θ[1] * v[1] + θ[3] * v[2] + θ[5])
    h2 = tanh(θ[2] * v[1] + θ[4] * v[2] + θ[6])
    return typeof(v)(θ[7] * h1 + θ[9] * h2 + θ[11], θ[8] * h1 + θ[10] * h2 + θ[12])
end
for _ in 1:8
    u = @SVector rand(Float32, 2)
    @assert sc(u, p_static) ≈ mlp(u, p_static)
end

# Training loss (Adam, L-BFGS, SerialPSO): adaptive Tsit5 in Float32, same tolerances as GPU-PSO.
nn_ode(u, p, t) = convert(typeof(u), sc(u, p))
sprob_nn = ODEProblem(nn_ode, U0, TSPAN)
function loss_tsit5(p)
    sol = solve(
        sprob_nn, Tsit5(); p, saveat = tsteps, abstol = 1.0f-6, reltol = 1.0f-5,
        sensealg = QuadratureAdjoint(autojacvec = ZygoteVJP())
    )
    SciMLBase.successful_retcode(sol) || return Inf32
    return sum(abs2, data .- Array(sol))
end

asvec(u) = u isa AbstractVector ? u : u[]

# Reference loss: Float64, tight tolerances. Scores only; nothing trains on it.
const DATA64 = Float64.(data)
const TSTEPS64 = Float64.(tsteps)
ref_ode(u, p, t) = mlp(u, p)
ref_prob = ODEProblem{false}(ref_ode, Float64.(U0), Float64.(TSPAN), SVector{NPARAMS, Float64}(p_nn...))
function ref_solve(u)
    p = SVector{NPARAMS, Float64}(Float64.(asvec(u))...)
    return Array(solve(ref_prob, Tsit5(); p, saveat = TSTEPS64, abstol = 1.0e-10, reltol = 1.0e-10))
end
common(u) = sum(abs2, DATA64 .- ref_solve(u))

lb_s = @SVector fill(-BOX, NPARAMS)
ub_s = @SVector fill(BOX, NPARAMS)

@printf "Training loss at start: %.4f   Reference loss at start: %.4f\n" loss_tsit5(p_static) common(p_static)
```

## Optimizers

Every runner takes a deadline and returns `(; u, own, iters, evals)`.

### Gradient-based

```julia
optf = OptimizationFunction((x, p) -> loss_tsit5(x), Optimization.AutoZygote())
optprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, p_nn)
moptprob = OptimizationProblem(optf, MArray{Tuple{NPARAMS}}(p_nn...))

function run_gradient(prob, opt, deadline; evals)
    n = Ref(0)
    cb = (state, l) -> (n[] += 1; time() >= deadline)
    sol = solve(prob, opt; maxiters = 10^7, callback = cb)
    u = asvec(sol.u)
    return (; u, own = Float64(loss_tsit5(u)), iters = n[], evals = evals(sol, n[]))
end
run_adam(deadline) = run_gradient(optprob, Adam(0.05), deadline; evals = (sol, n) -> n)
run_lbfgs(deadline) = run_gradient(moptprob, LBFGS(), deadline; evals = (sol, n) -> sol.original.f_calls)
```

### SerialPSO

```julia
const NEVALS = Ref(0)
counted_loss(x, p) = (NEVALS[] += 1; loss_tsit5(x))
s_prob = OptimizationProblem{false}(
    OptimizationFunction{false}(counted_loss, SciMLBase.NoAD()),
    p_static, nothing; lb = lb_s, ub = ub_s
)

serial_solve(m) = solve(s_prob, ParallelParticleSwarms.SerialPSO(NPART_CPU); maxiters = m)

const SERIAL_ITERS = Ref(1)                     # set in warm-up
function run_serial(deadline)
    NEVALS[] = 0
    sol = serial_solve(SERIAL_ITERS[])
    return (; u = asvec(sol.u), own = Float64(first(sol.objective)), iters = SERIAL_ITERS[], evals = NEVALS[])
end
```

### CPU global baselines

Same training loss and box as `SerialPSO`; both stop on `maxtime`. The problem is in
`Float64` because NLopt works in `Float64`; candidates are converted back to `Float32`, so
the loss is exactly the one Adam and L-BFGS train on.

```julia
box_prob = OptimizationProblem(
    OptimizationFunction((x, p) -> (NEVALS[] += 1; Float64(loss_tsit5(Float32.(x))))),
    Float64.(p_nn); lb = fill(Float64(-BOX), NPARAMS), ub = fill(Float64(BOX), NPARAMS)
)
function run_global(opt, deadline)
    NEVALS[] = 0
    sol = solve(box_prob, opt; maxtime = deadline - time(), maxiters = 10^7)
    return (; u = Float32.(sol.u), own = Float64(sol.objective), iters = missing, evals = NEVALS[])
end
run_de(deadline) = run_global(BBO_adaptive_de_rand_1_bin_radiuslimited(), deadline)
run_crs2(deadline) = run_global(NLopt.GN_CRS2_LM(), deadline)
```

### GPU-PSO through `parameter_estim_ode!` (the paper's method)

Each iteration solves all particles' ODEs as one GPU ensemble (adaptive `GPUTsit5`) and
reduces the loss on device.

```julia
nn_fn(u, p, t) = mlp(u, p)
prob_nn = ODEProblem{false}(nn_fn, U0, TSPAN, p_static)
soptprob = OptimizationProblem((u, p) -> eltype(u)(Inf), p_static, nothing; lb = lb_s, ub = ub_s)  # box only
gpu_data = adapt(BACKEND, [SVector{2, Float32}(@view data[:, i]) for i in 1:DATASIZE])
improb = DiffEqGPU.make_prob_compatible(prob_nn)
prob_func(prob, particle) = remake(prob, p = particle.position)

function make_cache()
    gbest, particles = ParallelParticleSwarms.init_particles(
        soptprob, ParallelParticleSwarms.ParallelPSOKernel(NPART_GPU), typeof(p_static)
    )
    return (;
        losses = adapt(BACKEND, ones(Float32, NPART_GPU)), gpu_particles = adapt(BACKEND, particles),
        gpu_data, gbest, probs = adapt(BACKEND, fill(improb, NPART_GPU)),
    )
end

function run_gpupso(deadline)
    cache, iters = make_cache(), 0
    gbest = cache.gbest
    while time() < deadline
        gbest = ParallelParticleSwarms.parameter_estim_ode!(
            prob_nn, cache, lb_s, ub_s, Val(true);
            saveat = tsteps, dt = 0.01f0, abstol = 1.0f-6, reltol = 1.0f-5, maxiters = CHUNK_GPUPSO, prob_func
        )
        cache = (; cache..., gbest)
        iters += CHUNK_GPUPSO
    end
    return (; u = gbest.position, own = Float64(gbest.cost), iters, evals = iters * NPART_GPU)
end
```

### `SyncPSOKernel` and `HybridPSO-LBFGS` on a kernel-compatible loss

These evaluate the objective inside GPU kernels, so the ODE solve is a fixed-step RK4 with
`NSUB` substeps per data interval. Fixed-step error grows with |θ| and a swarm will exploit
it, so `NSUB` is set high and the agreement check after the runs validates it. `HybridPSO`
polishes every particle with L-BFGS after the swarm.

```julia
@inline function rk4(u, θ, h)
    k1 = mlp(u, θ);                k2 = mlp(u .+ h / 2 .* k1, θ)
    k3 = mlp(u .+ h / 2 .* k2, θ); k4 = mlp(u .+ h .* k3, θ)
    return u .+ h / 6 .* (k1 .+ 2 .* k2 .+ 2 .* k3 .+ k4)
end

const NSUB = 32

struct RK4Loss{U, D, T}   # constants live here; `p = nothing` is required by the in-kernel L-BFGS
    u0::U; data::D; h::T
end
function (l::RK4Loss)(θ, p)
    u = SVector{2, eltype(θ)}(l.u0)
    loss = sum(abs2, u .- l.data[1])
    for i in 2:DATASIZE
        for _ in 1:NSUB
            u = rk4(u, θ, l.h)
        end
        loss += sum(abs2, u .- l.data[i])
    end
    return loss
end

loss_rk4 = RK4Loss(U0, ntuple(i -> SVector{2, Float32}(data[:, i]), DATASIZE), Float32(step(tsteps)) / NSUB)
k_prob = OptimizationProblem{false}(
    OptimizationFunction{false}(loss_rk4, SciMLBase.NoAD()),
    p_static, nothing; lb = lb_s, ub = ub_s
)

sync_opt = ParallelParticleSwarms.ParallelSyncPSOKernel(NPART_GPU; backend = BACKEND)
sync_local_opt = ParallelParticleSwarms.ParallelSyncPSOKernel(NPART_GPU; backend = BACKEND, local_best = true)
hybrid_opt = ParallelParticleSwarms.HybridPSO(; backend = BACKEND, pso = sync_opt)
const HYBRID_OPTS = Dict(                     # HybridPSO rows run with run_hybrid
    "HybridPSO-LBFGS" => hybrid_opt,
    "HybridPSO local-best" => ParallelParticleSwarms.HybridPSO(; backend = BACKEND, pso = sync_local_opt),
    "HybridPSO-LBFGS+NM" => ParallelParticleSwarms.HybridPSO(; backend = BACKEND, pso = sync_opt, polish = NelderMeadPolish(; maxiters = 1_000)),
)

function chunked!(cache, deadline)            # advance a kernel-PSO cache until the deadline
    iters, sol = 0, nothing
    while sol === nothing || time() < deadline
        sol = SciMLBase.solve!(cache; maxiters = CHUNK_KERNEL)
        cache.gbest = ParallelParticleSwarms.SPSOGBest(sol.u, first(sol.objective))
        iters += CHUNK_KERNEL
    end
    return sol, iters
end
result(sol, iters, evals) = (; u = asvec(sol.u), own = Float64(first(sol.objective)), iters, evals)

function run_sync(opt)
    return function (deadline)
        sol, iters = chunked!(SciMLBase.init(k_prob, opt), deadline)
        return result(sol, iters, iters * NPART_GPU)
    end
end

polish(cache) = SciMLBase.solve!(cache; maxiters = 0, local_maxiters = LOCAL_MAXITERS, abstol = 1.0f-8, reltol = 1.0f-8)
const POLISH_TIME = Dict{String, Float64}()   # set in warm-up, per HybridPSO row
function run_hybrid(name)
    return function (deadline)
        cache = SciMLBase.init(k_prob, HYBRID_OPTS[name])
        _, iters = chunked!(cache.pso_cache, deadline - POLISH_TIME[name])
        return result(polish(cache), iters, missing)
    end
end

const RESTART_SWARM_ITERS, RESTART_LOCAL_ITERS = 100, 100
one_restart() = solve(
    k_prob, hybrid_opt; maxiters = RESTART_SWARM_ITERS,
    local_maxiters = RESTART_LOCAL_ITERS, abstol = 1.0f-8, reltol = 1.0f-8
)
const RESTART_TIME = Ref(0.0)                 # set in warm-up
function run_hybrid_restarts(deadline)        # fresh swarm each restart; keep the best
    best, n = nothing, 0
    while time() + RESTART_TIME[] < deadline
        sol = one_restart(); n += 1
        (best === nothing || first(sol.objective) < first(best.objective)) && (best = sol)
    end
    return result(best, n * RESTART_SWARM_ITERS, missing)
end
```

## Runs

One warm-up per optimizer absorbs compilation; for `HybridPSO` it runs a full-budget swarm
and times the polish on it, for `HybridPSO (restarts)` it times one restart, and for
`SerialPSO` it calibrates the iteration count. Each seed then gets one run against the
budget; the recorded solution is the median-loss run.

```julia
results = Dict{String, Any}()

function warmup!(name, run)
    Random.seed!(0)
    return if haskey(HYBRID_OPTS, name)
        cache = SciMLBase.init(k_prob, HYBRID_OPTS[name])
        chunked!(cache.pso_cache, time() + BUDGET)    # full-length swarm, as in the real runs
        polish(cache)
        POLISH_TIME[name] = @elapsed polish(cache)
        @assert POLISH_TIME[name] < BUDGET "the $name polish does not fit in the budget"
    elseif name == "HybridPSO (restarts)"
        one_restart()
        RESTART_TIME[] = @elapsed one_restart()
        @assert RESTART_TIME[] < BUDGET "one HybridPSO restart does not fit in the budget"
    elseif name == "SerialPSO"
        serial_solve(CAL_ITERS)
        t = @elapsed serial_solve(CAL_ITERS)
        m = max(1, round(Int, BUDGET / (t / CAL_ITERS)))
        t = @elapsed serial_solve(m)
        SERIAL_ITERS[] = max(1, round(Int, m * BUDGET / t))
    else
        run(time() + 2.0)
    end
end

function record!(name, run)
    warmup!(name, run)
    rs, losses, times = [], Float64[], Float64[]
    for s in SEEDS
        Random.seed!(s)
        t = @elapsed r = run(time() + BUDGET)
        push!(rs, r); push!(losses, common(r.u)); push!(times, t)
    end
    i = sortperm(losses)[(length(losses) + 1) ÷ 2]
    return results[name] = (; rs[i]..., losses, time = median(times))
end

record!("Adam", run_adam)
record!("L-BFGS", run_lbfgs)
record!("BBO adaptive DE", run_de)
record!("NLopt CRS2", run_crs2)
record!("SerialPSO", run_serial)
record!("GPU-PSO", run_gpupso)
record!("SyncPSOKernel", run_sync(sync_opt))
record!("HybridPSO-LBFGS", run_hybrid("HybridPSO-LBFGS"))
record!("HybridPSO (restarts)", run_hybrid_restarts)
record!("SyncPSO local-best", run_sync(sync_local_opt))
record!("HybridPSO local-best", run_hybrid("HybridPSO local-best"))
record!("HybridPSO-LBFGS+NM", run_hybrid("HybridPSO-LBFGS+NM"))
nothing
```

## Results

```julia
const ORDER = [
    "Adam", "L-BFGS", "BBO adaptive DE", "NLopt CRS2", "SerialPSO", "GPU-PSO",
    "SyncPSOKernel", "HybridPSO-LBFGS", "HybridPSO (restarts)",
    "SyncPSO local-best", "HybridPSO local-best", "HybridPSO-LBFGS+NM",
]

@printf "budget per run: %.0f s, %d seeds; particles: GPU %d, Serial %d\n\n" BUDGET length(SEEDS) NPART_GPU NPART_CPU
println(
    rpad("optimizer", 22), lpad("median", 9), lpad("best", 9), lpad("worst", 9),
    lpad("own", 9), lpad("iters", 7), lpad("evals", 10), lpad("time(s)", 8)
)
for name in ORDER
    r = results[name]
    na(x) = ismissing(x) ? "n/a" : string(x)
    @printf "%-22s%9.4f%9.4f%9.4f%9.4f%7s%10s%8.1f\n" name median(r.losses) minimum(r.losses) maximum(r.losses) r.own na(r.iters) na(r.evals) r.time
end
```

### Integrator agreement

Own objective vs. reference loss at the median run's returned parameters. A gap above 10%
means the optimizer was minimizing solver error rather than the ODE fit.

```julia
const GAP_TOL = 0.1
println(rpad("optimizer", 22), lpad("own", 12), lpad("reference", 12), lpad("rel. gap", 10))
for name in ORDER
    r = results[name]
    ref = common(r.u)
    gap = abs(r.own - ref) / ref
    @printf "%-22s%12.4f%12.4f%9.1f%%%s\n" name r.own ref 100gap (gap > GAP_TOL ? "   <-- exceeds tolerance" : "")
end
```

### Fitted trajectories

```julia
fig = Figure(size = (1000, 480))
ax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel = "t", ylabel = "u₁(t)", title = @sprintf("Fit after %.0f s of wall clock", BUDGET))
scatter!(ax, tsteps, data[1, :]; label = "data", color = :black, markersize = 12)
markers = [
    :circle, :rect, :utriangle, :diamond, :star5, :xcross,
    :hexagon, :cross, :ltriangle, :rtriangle, :pentagon, :dtriangle,
]
for (i, name) in enumerate(ORDER)
    scatterlines!(
        ax, tsteps, ref_solve(results[name].u)[1, :]; label = name,
        marker = markers[i], markersize = 8, linewidth = 1.5
    )
end
Legend(fig[1, 2], ax; framevisible = false)
fig
```

### Common loss across seeds

```julia
fig = Figure(size = (800, 480))
ax = Axis(
    fig[1, 1]; yscale = log10, ylabel = "Common loss (reference Tsit5, Float64)",
    xticks = (1:length(ORDER), ORDER), xticklabelrotation = pi / 6
)
for (i, name) in enumerate(ORDER)
    l = results[name].losses
    scatter!(ax, fill(i, length(l)), max.(l, 1.0e-8); marker = markers[i], markersize = 14)
end
fig
```

